## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Future Careers – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Careers</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> To Become a Doctor, First I Will Need To...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Create a short narrative based on a personal experience. Interactive: Provide feedback on short texts during a peer review session.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a short description of their future career plans by listing their motivation and planned steps in sequence (e.g., "I want to be a doctor because I like helping people. First, I will study medicine. Next, I will gain experience by training at a hospital.").
Can provide feedback on a peer's narrative about career plans (e.g., "Good job describing your future goals!").
Can clarify different job roles by writing simple descriptions supported with pictures for common tasks (e.g., "A doctor helps people get healthy.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by asking students to think about different jobs. Students write down three different jobs they know and one thing a person in that job *does*. For example, "A doctor helps sick people." "A teacher helps students learn." Then, students share one of their sentences with a partner.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing a sentence about a future career goal on the board, emphasizing "I want to + infinitive" and "I will need to." For example, the teacher writes: "I want to be a professional lawyer because I like to help people. I will need to study hard and practice a lot." The teacher explicitly pronounces the modals 'will' and 'would' with correct /ɪ/ and /ʊ/ sounds, and briefly highlights the difference between /ʃ/ in "should" and /tʃ/ in "teach" if applicable to words used.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the target grammar and concepts.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'I want to be a lawyer,' am I talking about my past, present, or future plans?" (Expected answer: Future plans/intentions)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I will need to study hard,' does it mean it's something I *must* do or something I *might* do?" (Expected answer: Must do/important for my plan)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Future Careers."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher displays a short narrative text (e.g., on a projector or handout) written by a fictional 8th-grade student about their future career plans. The narrative incorporates target grammar (e.g., "I want to be...", "I will need to...", "I would like to...") and vocabulary (doctor, plan, study, achieve, succeed, prepare, important, professional). The text describes motivation and sequential steps.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Text:</b> "My name is Ana, and I want to be a skilled doctor in the future. I would like to help people get healthy. First, I will need to finish high school successfully. Next, I plan to study medicine at the university. After that, I will train at a hospital and gain practical experience. To achieve my goals, I will prepare for many exams and practice hard. It is an important and professional career."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Production of short, simple written texts on familiar topics and personal interests (e.g., future career plans).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the text silently. Then, the teacher asks gist questions for written responses:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What career does Ana want to pursue?" (Students write their answer)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List two things Ana will need to do to achieve her goal." (Students write their answer)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences and short paragraphs using future tense for plans (e.g., "I will need to..."), modals for intention/suggestions (e.g., "I would like to..."), and "want to + infinitive." Focus on integrating vocabulary: doctor, lawyer, teacher, career, goals, internship, qualifications, plan, practice, train, achieve, succeed, prepare, professional, skilled, important, successfully, professionally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Cloze):</b> The teacher provides a handout with sentences containing blanks related to career plans. Students complete the sentences by writing the correct target vocabulary or grammar structure.</li>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "My sister ______ to be a ______. She will ______ to get good ______." (Answers: wants, doctor, need, qualifications)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "What ______ you want to be? I ______ like to be a ______." (Answers: do, would, lawyer)</li>
                        </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentence beginnings or prompts. Students complete them in writing, ensuring they use future plans and intentions.</li>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example prompts: "To become a skilled engineer, I will need to...", "My friend wants to be a professional ____ because...", "After high school, I plan to...". Students write full sentences in their notebooks.</li>
                        </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Writing & Peer Check):</b> In pairs, students orally discuss their partner's career goals using Wh-questions (e.g., "What do you want to be in the future?"), then each student writes 2-3 sentences summarizing their partner's plans. They exchange their written summaries with their partner for a quick review of clarity and grammar (e.g., "Good job describing my future goals!"). This activity promotes pragmatic functions of asking about and expressing future goals.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Future Careers."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual written narrative composition.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> Students will write a short narrative paragraph (5-7 sentences) about their own future career plans. They must include their motivation for choosing that career and outline at least three sequential steps they will take to achieve their goals. Students are encouraged to use the target grammar (e.g., "I want to be...", "I will need to...", "I would like to...") and vocabulary (doctor, lawyer, teacher, career, goals, internship, qualifications, plan, practice, train, achieve, succeed, prepare, professional, skilled, important, successfully, professionally).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Actions:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Provide a clear prompt on the board: "Write a short paragraph about what you want to be in the future. Why do you want to be that? What will you need to do to achieve your goals? Remember to use phrases like 'First, I will need to...', 'Next, I plan to...', 'Finally, I would like to...'. "</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Circulate the classroom, providing individual support, prompting for more detail, and checking for correct use of target language. Remind students to focus on writing clear and coherent sentences.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Peer Review Session. Students exchange their written narratives (from Stage 4) with a partner. Each student reads their partner's narrative and provides constructive feedback using a simple checklist provided by the teacher. This task directly addresses the interactive writing objective: "Provide feedback on short texts during a peer review session."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the narrative clearly state the career goal? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the writer use "I want to be..." or "I would like to be..."? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the writer list at least two steps using "I will need to..." or similar phrases? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the narrative include at least three vocabulary words from the list (e.g., doctor, plan, train, achieve)? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Is the text easy to understand? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">*Optional suggestion for peer: "Good job!" or "Maybe you can add..."*</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I learn about planning for my future career today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing about my career plans was easy or difficult for me?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well do I feel I expressed my future career plans in writing today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What grammatical structures (e.g., 'I want to be...', 'I will need to...') did I use most effectively in my writing, and what could I improve?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., the peer review activity encouraged active engagement, students successfully used 'want to + infinitive' and 'will need to' in their narratives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges arose? (e.g., some students struggled with sequencing their steps logically, minor errors in subject-verb agreement in writing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., provide sentence starters for sequencing, a quick review of basic sentence structure in the warm-up, focus on clearer instructions for the peer feedback).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their written narratives as a basis for a collaborative project. They might present their plans, create a visual aid, or summarize a peer's plans, thus mediating information from their written work into a different format.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>